1
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:11,620
In this lesson, we'll introduce you to input metrics and explain how we realized their

2
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:15,340
importance at Amazon and how it changed the way we manage the business.

3
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:21,120
Jeff and I once visited a Fortune 500 company to meet privately with the CEO in his office.

4
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:25,520
During our meeting, an assistant dashed in and handed the boss a sheet of paper.

5
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:29,260
The CEO glanced at it and waved at it and proudly said,

6
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:34,980
our stock is up 30 cents this morning. He perked up as if he had personally caused the rise.

7
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:40,580
As we drove to our next meeting, Jeff said, there's nothing the CEO did to cause that 30

8
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:45,540
cent blip in the stock price. I agreed and added that I wouldn't be surprised if the assistant

9
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:50,140
had thrown multiple printouts in the recycle bin that morning when the blip wasn't so big.

10
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:54,020
Would the same scene have played out if the share price had dropped 30 cents?

11
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:55,500
The more proficient the CEO was, the more likely he would have been to win.

12
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:59,220
The profound lesson is that share price is what Amazon calls an output metric.

13
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:04,440
In this chapter, we'll introduce the concept of input metrics, which will enable you to focus

14
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:08,300
on the activities that drive your business in a meaningful and positive direction.

15
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:13,460
The CEO I just mentioned and companies in general can rarely sustainably control

16
00:01:13,460 --> 00:01:18,680
output metrics directly. What's important is to focus on the controllable input metrics,

17
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:24,320
the activities you directly control, which ultimately affect output metrics such as share

18
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:24,720
price.

19
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:30,920
Input metrics are controllable and usually customer-facing. Some companies refer to them

20
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:37,100
as leading indicators. We like to use a stronger term, controllable input metrics. That means it's

21
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:41,720
a conscious choice you make whether to devote resources, whether those resources are people,

22
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:46,640
equipment, software, marketing, spending, geographic expansion, or something else,

23
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:52,240
to move the metrics in the desired direction. Thus, input metrics are things that, when done

24
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:55,480
right, improve the customer experience, create value, and improve the business.

25
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:55,980
Input metrics are things that, when done right, improve the business. Thus, input metrics are things that, when done right,

26
00:01:55,980 --> 00:02:00,680
create value, and bring about the desired results in your output metrics like sales, profit, and share price.

27
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:06,940
Your team's actions, organizational decisions, and where and how you invest money are controllable

28
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:12,580
inputs. Input metrics are ways to measure the impact and status of all these elements that you

29
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:18,620
control. Output metrics are lagging indicators. They measure the results of the inputs, which are

30
00:02:18,620 --> 00:02:23,820
leading indicators. Output metrics include financial metrics like revenue, gross profit,

31
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:24,880
and free cash flow.

32
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:30,780
As well as metrics like active customers, new subscribers, and churn rate. They're very important,

33
00:02:31,060 --> 00:02:36,240
but they generally can't be directly manipulated in a sustainable way over a long period of time.

34
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:43,080
Most, but not all, input metrics are customer-facing. Customer-facing input metrics measure factors

35
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:48,500
that positively improve the customer experience. Factors that make your service or product better,

36
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:53,560
faster, cheaper, or some combination of all three. Each sustained improvement in the customer

37
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:55,460
experience creates long-term value for the customer. And that's why input metrics are

38
00:02:55,480 --> 00:03:01,420
for customers. It pays dividends for the next several years. While most input metrics are

39
00:03:01,420 --> 00:03:06,760
customer-facing, many do not directly relate to the customer experience. An example of an input

40
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:12,140
metric that's not customer-facing and that we paid close attention to at Amazon is inventory

41
00:03:12,140 --> 00:03:19,140
record accuracy, or IRA percentage. 100% inventory record accuracy means that all of the retail

42
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:25,120
goods, item quantity, and bin location in the fulfillment center perfectly match what's in the

43
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:25,460
electronic store. So, if you're a customer, you're going to have to pay close attention to the

44
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:31,120
records of the warehouse management system. When Amazon accepts a customer order, the warehouse

45
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:37,540
management system must accurately indicate the in-stock status and location, which fulfillment

46
00:03:37,540 --> 00:03:42,700
center, aisle, shelf, and bin location to fulfill the items within the promised delivery date and

47
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:48,600
time. As a result, a lower IRA percentage will result in a lower rate of on-time order fulfillment,

48
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:54,920
higher inventory costs, and higher fulfillment costs. Some other examples of input metrics that

49
00:03:54,920 --> 00:03:55,120
are not customer-facing are the inventory record accuracy, or the warehouse management system.

50
00:03:55,120 --> 00:03:58,600
The metrics that are not customer-facing include average days accounts payable,

51
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:04,460
workplace safety incidents, online marketing dollars spent per month. You may have noticed

52
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:09,920
that many of these metrics will indirectly affect customers. For example, inventory record accuracy

53
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:16,080
appears to be a purely operational internal metric. However, when you consider the implications of a

54
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:21,060
low IRA percentage, not knowing whether you have an item or not, or where it is located,

55
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:24,520
this would have a significant negative impact on customers.

56
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:30,300
At Amazon, we took to heart the lesson that we could only forecast and observe our outputs,

57
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:35,480
but we had a high degree of control over the inputs. This realization was the cumulative

58
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:40,520
effect of years of operating experience. Our efforts to hit revenue targets at the end of

59
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:44,720
each quarter and our first few years as a public company firmly informed this.

60
00:04:45,460 --> 00:04:49,000
If we are trending to miss our financial goals as we neared the end of the quarter,

61
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:54,860
a fire drill would ensue. Finance and category leaders would send frantic emails leading to

62
00:04:55,120 --> 00:05:00,780
meetings to discuss emergency plans to hit our goals. Then teams would scramble to enact these

63
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:05,500
hastily conceived ideas to boost revenue and profit for the quarter's remaining few weeks.

64
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:10,480
Most of our activities involved promotions, slashing prices, and getting rid of excess

65
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:16,160
inventory. Sometimes these promotions provided a small boost to sales, but in most cases,

66
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:20,880
their real impact was that they distracted us from improving the customer experience,

67
00:05:21,140 --> 00:05:25,000
the long-term driver of growth. We learned that instead of reacting to the

68
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:25,100
outcomes of our actions, we should be more focused on the results of our actions.

69
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:31,000
We should focus on controlling the inputs. This approach is one of the reasons why Amazon is now

70
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:36,580
one of the world's most valuable companies. Every process emits lots of data, whether you

71
00:05:36,580 --> 00:05:41,580
measured or not. Most teams do a good job measuring a process's output. We've noticed

72
00:05:41,580 --> 00:05:45,900
that most people do not do nearly as well measuring their inputs, which means they're

73
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:50,720
at a disadvantage. In the next few sections, we'll show you how you can define and discover

74
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:55,000
your inputs. Thanks for watching today. You can follow us on LinkedIn or visit us at

75
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:00,340
visit our website, workingbackwards.com, to find more content in our library that's part of this course.